# Installer, uninstaller and systemd unit

- Windows is for development only. The daemon warns at startup that data-directory ACLs are unchecked on Windows.
- `scripts/install.sh` runs as root on other people's Ubuntu/Debian hosts (amd64, arm64, systemd), usually piped from curl: no prompts, everything in functions with one grouped call on the last line (a truncated download must be a syntax error), commands that could read stdin get `</dev/null`. Its `readonly RELEASE_VERSION=''` line is stamped by `scripts/release.sh`; keep it exactly that.
- Order: validate options (the TLS files are copied into the private work directory first; those copies are checked, pair matched with `openssl` when present, and installed), then every host check (root, OS, arch, systemd, no directory at a fixed destination, ports, Docker state, Docker's apt files and the release code name when the repository is to be added, download, SHA-256, archive contents, binary runs and reports the expected version) before the first change. A failed download or checksum must leave the host untouched. What can still fail after changes began (packages, Docker's key or candidates, the service start) is listed in `docs/INSTALL.md`; keep that list true. Payloads come from a release download (`SHA256SUMS` or `--sha256`), `--archive` (refused without `--sha256` or a `SHA256SUMS` entry next to it; copied into the work directory first with the size cap enforced while writing, and only that copy is hashed, listed and extracted) or `--binary` (the unverified path for source checkouts; unit from `deploy/`, uninstaller from `scripts/`); all are installed from a private `mktemp -d`. Archives must hold exactly `LICENSE golem-deploy golem-deploy.service uninstall.sh` as plain files (sizes from `tar --numeric-owner -tv`, parsed strictly); modes are always set by the installer, never taken from the archive. `GOLEM_DEPLOY_RELEASE_URL` (https or file) replaces the download directory. curl: `-q` first (no `~/.curlrc`), `--proto =https --proto-redir =https --tlsv1.2`, retries in `fetch`, and size caps enforced while writing (`head -c`), because older curl ignores `--max-filesize` without a length.
- Docker: a working Docker is kept. Missing: Docker's apt repository per Docker's install pages (`/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc`, deb822 `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.sources` with `Signed-By`), written only after the key's fingerprint matched (always checked; the packages step installs `gpg` when missing), and never over existing files: an existing but disabled `docker.sources` stops the host checks, a keyring with other content stops before anything is written for Docker. An existing repository is reused; `docker_repo_configured` reads sources as apt does: enabled entries in `sources.list`, `*.list` (text after `#` ignored, the URI field compared) and `*.sources` (`Enabled:` no/false/0/off and the like in any case disable a stanza), no backups. After `apt-get update`, `docker_candidates_ok` takes the sources `apt-cache policy` lists under each candidate version and requires every one with the highest priority to be exactly `https://download.docker.com/linux/<os>`, on the full and the buildx-only path. Packages `docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin`; every `apt-get install` uses `--no-remove`. Stopped `docker-ce`: start it. Conflicting distribution packages with no working Docker: stop with the `apt-get remove` command, never remove packages. Missing buildx: install `docker-buildx-plugin` only with `docker-ce` and Docker's repository. `--no-docker` disables all installs. Snap and rootless Docker stay unsupported. Keep the conflict list and steps in sync with Docker's pages.
- Idempotent: a re-run replaces binary, unit, uninstaller and TLS copies, rewrites `/etc/sysctl.d/60-golem-deploy.conf` (never lowering a higher value; verify by reading `/proc/sys`, since `sysctl -p` exits 0 on refusal), resets the unit's start limit and restarts (fixed destinations with `install -T`/`mv -T`; the restart's status is checked, and a failing `systemctl` never ends the script before the failure handling); it never rewrites an existing env file or touches an existing data directory (it refuses one with the wrong owner or mode). When the service does not start and this run wrote the env file, it stops the service and moves the file to `golem-deploy.env.failed`.
- A fresh install with neither listen override nor TLS files uses 443 plus an HTTP listener on 80 when both are free, otherwise a loopback port. Without `--public-host`, a public outgoing address from `ip route get` becomes `GOLEM_DEPLOY_PUBLIC_HOST`; private addresses leave it empty with a hint. The env file holds only `GOLEM_DEPLOY_*` variables of `serve` (`cmd/golem-deploy/main.go`). When a serve flag, its default, the data directory rules (`internal/datadir`), the plaintext/loopback rule or the port-range rules change, update the script's validation, its printed next steps and `docs/INSTALL.md`.
- Next steps come from the effective env file, including the data directory for `admin setup-link` and the printed commands: only a plain line `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DATA_DIR=/path` the daemon accepts (`settings_data_dir`, same rules in `uninstall.sh`), passed as one argument and shell-quoted when printed; otherwise no link, no fallback to the default directory, and a `<data directory>` placeholder. An active service that never logged `golem-deploy listening` gets no link either. The first admin step relies on `golem-deploy admin setup-link [--data-dir] [--origin] [--ttl]` (one line `<origin>/setup#t=<token>` on stdout; exit 0, 3 admin exists, 1 error, 2 usage or an older binary), run as the service user after the service started. The link is a secret: keep it in a variable, never in a file or the journal; accept only a 43-character `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` token; turn `xtrace` off from the link's creation to the end of `main` and restore it. Pipelines under `pipefail` must not let a reader exit early (no `head` or `grep -q` after a writer that could get SIGPIPE). Fallback: the `admin create` command (must match its flags, run as the service user). The managed-TLS fingerprint is read from this start's journal line `panel self-signed TLS fingerprint` (`internal/certs/panel.go`); keep the parser in sync. It never changes firewall rules; with ufw active it prints `ufw allow` lines.
- `scripts/uninstall.sh` (installed as `/usr/local/sbin/golem-deploy-uninstall`) surveys first (refusing an unreadable `/proc/self/mountinfo` and mounts at or below a directory it deletes), then stops the service and requires `systemctl show` to say the unit is stopped or not found and no `serve` to hold `<data-dir>/.lock` (tested with `flock --shared --nonblock` on a regular file, never created), then (purge) lists and deletes Docker objects, checking every listing's exit status (no unchecked pipes in substitutions; bash does the filtering), then checks mounts again and removes cache, data and settings directories, unit, binary, sysctl file and user, and itself only when nothing failed. Every `docker` call goes through a function that uses `--host unix:///var/run/docker.sock` without `DOCKER_*` variables. A failed listing or deletion keeps everything else and prints the retry command (path quoted, `--instance`, `--yes` kept, a piped copy told to save `uninstall.sh`). Docker objects are deleted only with a known instance id: `--instance`, else the installed binary's `admin instance-id` (run as the service user, accepted only with exit 0; 1 not initialised, 2 usage or an older binary) for the data directory the settings give the service (`settings_data_dir`; the default directory only without a settings file; an unusable value means unknown); unknown with managed objects present (images listed with `--all`) stops and lists the ids with counts. Containers: managed and instance labels plus any of the daemon-only keys `server-id`, `inspector-hub`, `helper` (union of queries; build images never carry them). Images: only `golem-deploy/` tags with `--no-prune`, each checked right before removal to still name the selected image id; never removal by id, never `-f`; images left untagged, with other tags (derived images inherit our labels) or used by other containers are reported. A failed inspection counts as gone only when a listing confirms it. Files and links are removed with `rm -f` (a directory found there is left with a warning); directories with `rm -rf --one-file-system`. It prints what it will delete and needs a typed `purge` on a terminal or `--yes`. Never prune, never remove Docker, its repository or packages, never delete a data directory other than `/var/lib/golem-deploy`. It must cope with half-removed installations. Keep its labels and the image namespace in sync with `internal/docker` (`validate.go`, `data.go`, `build.go`), and the lock with `internal/datadir`.
- The unit's hardening must stay compatible with what the daemon does: run `git`, `git-lfs`, `ssh`, the docker CLI (including `docker build` through the buildx plugin, whose build client config and state are under `<data-dir>/docker-build` and scratch files in the private `/tmp`; the build runs in dockerd) and itself (askpass); connect to `/var/run/docker.sock`; bind TCP/UDP to probe game ports; write only below the data directory (plus the non-build `DOCKER_CONFIG` in the cache directory); pass env files to docker as an inherited pipe (`/dev/fd/3`). Every directive has a comment saying why; drop a directive rather than leave it unexplained. Paths handed to dockerd (bind sources) must be the real host paths, so no `TemporaryFileSystem=`/`BindPaths=` over the data directory and no `/tmp` paths. Game containers live in dockerd's cgroup: stopping the unit must never stop them, and `TimeoutStopSec` must cover the daemon's 20-second drain and store close (`internal/httpapi/server.go`, `internal/daemon/daemon.go`). The unit grants only `CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE`.
- Verification: `shellcheck scripts/*.sh` (CI job "Shell"); `systemd-analyze verify` on a changed unit; end to end in systemd-booted, privileged `ubuntu:24.04` and `debian:12` containers with volumes for the nested `/var/lib/docker` and `/var/lib/containerd` (the installer installs Docker inside), installing from `scripts/release.sh` output through `GOLEM_DEPLOY_RELEASE_URL=file://...`: checksum mismatch, truncated or tampered archives, Docker missing, `--no-docker`, re-runs, an existing env file, loopback and network listeners, every setup-link exit code (a small stand-in executable can force them), `--binary`, `--archive` (no checksum, a file swapped after the copy), a download without a length over the cap, Docker's key with `gpg` missing and a wrong key (a local `openssl s_server -WWW` behind an `/etc/hosts` entry and a trusted test CA), an apt pin that wins over Docker's repository, a local repository offering the same version at the same or a higher priority (full and buildx-only path), backup, disabled (`Enabled: 0`) or commented Docker sources, an existing disabled `docker.sources` or another keyring, an archive given as a link and one with a spaced owner name, TLS files swapped during the run, a service that is active but never listens, a directory at the binary's path, a data directory with a space and one in quotes, `insecure` in `~/.curlrc`, a failed first start, `bash -x`, uninstall, `--purge` with and without a terminal (`script -qec` gives one), with the id from `admin instance-id` (and a stand-in that fails), `--instance` and unknown, a quoted data directory with an older database in the default one, a daemon started by hand while the unit is stopped, an unknown unit state, `DOCKER_HOST` or a Docker context pointing elsewhere, an unreadable mount table and a mount added during the confirmation, the retry command with a spaced path, `--yes` and piped, a failed local removal, another instance's objects, an old container without `inspector-hub`, a failing Docker deletion and its retry, a mount below the data directory, operator-tagged, derived and untagged derived images (on the containerd and the classic image store), and a half-removed installation. Name test objects with a unique prefix, remove exactly those afterwards and never prune.
